In main, create a headless list with three elements 4,5,-12. Then, on consecutive lines, display the elements of the list. Use the structure:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};

In main, create a list with a head containing three elements 4,5,-12. Then, on consecutive lines, display the elements of the list. Use the structure:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};

14.2 Displaying

Write a function printListWithoutHead that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should display the consecutive elements of the list on consecutive lines or display a message that the list is empty. Create a test case.

Write a function printListWithHead that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should display the consecutive elements of the list on consecutive lines or display a message that the list is empty. Create a test case.

Write a function printPos that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should display those elements of the list that are positive. Create a test case.

Write a function printOdd that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should display those elements of the list that are odd. Create a test case.

14.3 Creating a list

Write a no-argument function createNoHead that returns a pointer to a “newly-created” empty headless list. Create a test case. Use the structure:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};

Write a no-argument function createWithHead that returns a pointer to a “newly-created” empty list with a head. Create a test case. Use the structure:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};

14.4 Adding at the beginning

Write a function addFirst that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
and an integer a. The function should add a new element with value a at the beginning of the list. Create a test case.

Write a function addFirst that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
and an integer a. The function should add a new element with value a at the beginning of the list. Create a test case.

14.5 Adding at the end

Write a function addLast that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
and an integer a. The function should add a new element with value a at the end of the list. Create a test case.

Write a function addLast that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
and an integer a. The function should add a new element with value a at the end of the list. Create a test case.

14.6 Searching/counters

Write a function find that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
and an integer a. The function should return 1 if at least one element of the list is equal to a, and return 0 otherwise. Create a test case.

Write a function find that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
and an integer a. The function should return 1 if at least one element of the list is equal to a, and return 0 otherwise. Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should return the sum of odd elements in the list. Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should return the sum of negative elements in the list. Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should return the address of the last even element or NULL if there is no such element or the list is empty. Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should return the address of the last positive element or NULL if there is no such element or the list is empty. Create a test case.

14.7 Removing individual elements

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should remove the first element of the list (provided the list is not empty). Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should remove the first element of the list (provided the list is not empty). Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should remove the last element of the list (provided the list is not empty). Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should remove the last element of the list (provided the list is not empty). Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should remove the second-to-last element of the list (provided the list has at least two elements). Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should remove the second-to-last element of the list (provided the list has at least two elements). Create a test case.

14.8 Removing the entire list

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should remove the entire list (manually, element by element). After removal, make sure the list is empty according to the convention. Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct element {
    int x;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should remove the entire list (manually, element by element). After removal, make sure the list is empty according to the convention. Create a test case.

14.9 Miscellaneous exercises

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct node {
    int i;
    struct node * next;
};
The function should display on the console, on consecutive lines, the values of those elements in the list that are perfect squares of integers. Create a test case.
Example. If the list consists of the elements 4,5,6,-34,0,25,11, then 4,0,25 should be displayed on consecutive lines.

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct node {
    double x;
    struct node * next;
};
and returns the smallest of the numbers in the list. In the case of an empty list, the function should return zero. Create one test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct node {
    int i;
    struct node * next;
};
The function should duplicate the value of the last element, provided the list is not empty. In the case of an empty list, the function should do nothing. Create a test case.
Example: For the list 3,4,5, the 5 should be duplicated, so after modification the list should have the form 3,4,5,5.

Write a function that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct elem {
    int a;
    struct elem * next;
};
The function should duplicate the value of the last element, provided the list is not empty. In the case of an empty list, the function should do nothing. Create a test case.
Example: For the list 3,4,5, the 5 should be duplicated, so after modification the list should have the form 3,4,5,5.

Write a function that compares two lists with a head with elements of type:

struct node {
    int i;
    struct node * next;
};
and returns 1 if the last elements of the lists are equal, and 0 otherwise (including when either of the lists or both are empty). Create one test case.

Write a function that compares two headless lists with elements of type:

struct node {
    int a;
    struct node * next;
};
and returns 1 if the lists are of the same length, and 0 otherwise (including when either of the lists or both are empty). Create a test case.

Write a function that takes two lists with a head with elements of type:

struct node {
    int value;
    struct node * next;
};
and returns 1 if the sum of all odd elements in both lists is the same, and 0 otherwise (including when either of the lists or both are empty). Create a test case.

Write a function that takes two headless lists with elements of type:

struct node {
    int w;
    struct node * next;
};
and returns 1 if the sum of all even elements in both lists is the same, and 0 otherwise (including when either of the lists or both are empty). Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct node {
    int x;
    struct node * next;
};
The function should return the sum of odd elements from the list. Create one test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct elem {
    int x;
    struct elem * next;
};
The function should double all positive elements in the list (if any exist). Create one test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct node {
    int y;
    struct node * next;
};
and two integers a and b. The function should add two new elements at the beginning of the list and set their values respectively from the given arguments. Create one test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct node {
    int a;
    struct node * next;
};
and two integers a and b. The function should add two new elements at the beginning of the list and set their values respectively from the given arguments. Create one test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a headless list with elements of type:

struct element {
    float value;
    struct element * next;
};
The function should replace each element’s value with its absolute value. For an empty list, the function should do nothing. Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct node {
    int val;
    struct node * next;
};
The function should replace each element’s value with its absolute value. For an empty list, the function should do nothing. Create a test case.

Write a function that takes as an argument a list with a head with elements of type:

struct node {
    int x;
    struct node * next;
};
and an integer d. The function should return how many elements in the list are equal to d. Create one test case.
